# Cursor Rules

## Location and naming

1. Place rule files in **`.cursor/rules/`** only (kebab-case, `.mdc` extension).
2. Never place rules in the project root or subdirectories outside `.cursor/rules/`.
3. See [README.md](README.md) for the full index of existing rules.

## Rule file format

```yaml
---
description: Short description (shown in rule picker)
globs: front-end/src/**/*.tsx   # omit when alwaysApply: true
alwaysApply: false
---
```

| Field | When to use |
|-------|-------------|
| `alwaysApply: true` | Repo-wide standards only (~5 rules). Do **not** also set globs. |
| `alwaysApply: false` + `globs` | Area-specific rules (front-end UI, backend routes, etc.). |

Keep each rule **under ~80 lines** when possible; split when a topic grows. Every rule should include a **Verification** section with exact commands (`npm run type-check`, `npm run lint`, etc.).

## When to add or update rules

**Add a new rule when:**
- A pattern appears in **3+ files** and keeps getting copied
- Common bugs could be prevented by a short guideline
- Code reviews repeat the same feedback

**Update an existing rule when:**
- Better reference implementations exist in the codebase
- Related rules or APIs changed
- Edge cases were discovered in production code
- Content was only linked from `spec/` — **inline the required details** into the rule instead of adding spec links

**Deprecate or remove when:**
- The pattern no longer exists in the codebase
- ESLint/CI now enforces the same constraint — keep the rule only if it adds project-specific context

## Self-contained — no `spec/` references

Rules under `.cursor/rules/` must **not** link to or depend on `spec/` files. Spec folders may be removed or reorganized; rules are the long-lived source of truth for agents.

**Do instead:**
- Inline the **minimum required** behavior, props, and patterns directly in the rule
- Point to **code** reference implementations (e.g. `ProfileMediaSelectorModal.tsx`, `CustomDialog.tsx`)
- Cross-link other **`.cursor/rules/`** files or **`.cursor/skills/`** when a topic is owned elsewhere

**Forbidden in rule files:**
- Links or paths like `spec/1.x.x/...`, `../../spec/...`
- “See spec for details” without copying the actionable requirement into the rule

When retiring spec content, migrate anything agents still need into the matching rule or skill — do not leave a spec-only pointer in `.cursor/rules/`.

## Quality checklist

- Actionable bullets, one concern per file
- Concrete examples from actual code paths (not abstract generics)
- Cross-link related **rules or skills** instead of duplicating content — never cross-link `spec/`
- Prefer `@/` path aliases in examples (see `code-cleanliness.mdc`)

## Continuous maintenance

After major refactors, audit `.cursor/rules/` against the codebase. Update the [README.md](README.md) index when adding or removing rules. Remove any `spec/` links surfaced during the audit.

For multi-step workflows (releases, showcase registration, new WebpageEditor addons), prefer **project skills** in `.cursor/skills/` over very long always-on rules.
